speechtext:
The provincial government were to guarantee the bonds for $25,000 a mile, and then the Dominion government was to grant aid at $6,400 a mile pursuant to the statute for a road between St.
135}
John and Grand Falls, where it was to connect with the Transcontinental. It was also agreed upon between the late Minister of Railways and the provincial government that aid was to be given for two large bridges across the St. John river, one across the river near Mistake, so-called, another at Andover and another across the ICennebecasis. Then the Intercolonial was to lease the line for a term of ninety-nine years. I may say that the late government took up this whole question of railway aid, and all the different propositions really formed part of one comprehensive scheme. We agreed, for instance, to build the Hudson Bay railway, we agreed to guarantee the bonds of the Canadian Northern so as to link up the eastern with the western .section. Then we made an agreement that the government could compel the Canadian Northern to exchange traffic with the Intercolonial at Montreal.
